        {
          "doi": "10.1007/s10835-026-09497-9",
          "title": "Books, Record Keeping, and a Rabbinical Career: Judah Mehler Reutlingen’s Self-Fashioning and Early Modern Ashkenazi Written Culture",
          "authors": "Ahituv, Omer",
          "journal_name": "Jewish History",
          "journal_abbrev": "Jewish Hist",
          "issn": "0334-701X",
          "volume": null,
          "issue": null,
          "pages": null,
          "abstract": "Abstract Early modern European cultures witnessed increased use of written materials and techniques, expanding the “repertoire of roles in written cultures.” These transformations significantly influenced early modern Ashkenazi culture. This article examines early modern Ashkenazi written culture through the case of Judah Mehler Reutlingen (1609–1659), rabbi of the small community of Bingen, and two manuscript editions of his compilation of homilies, Shevut Yehudah —the first composed before his rabbinical appointment and the second during his established career. Comparison of Reutlingen’s two editions demonstrates how he used elements of written culture, including record-keeping practices along with official documents and correspondence, to reshape his rabbinic persona as his professional status changed. Reutlingen’s self-fashioning strategies illuminate broader trends in early modern Ashkenazi culture—from the cultural perception of authorship, linking ḥiddushim (new insights) to the author as a manifestation of his soul, to the institutionalization of the rabbinate, and the development of rabbinic careers. This study shows the profound significance of written culture to early modern Ashkenazi, particularly rabbinic, culture, demonstrating how it shaped various cultural dimensions and served multiple functions.",
          "url": "https://doi.org/10.1007/s10835-026-09497-9",
          "published_online": "2026-08-18T00:00:00",
          "published_print": null,
          "published_date": "2026-08-18T00:00:00",
          "subjects": null,
          "article_type": "journal-article"
        },
        {
          "doi": "10.1007/s10835-026-09498-8",
          "title": "Wandering Through the Wonderland of Rabbinic Literature: Science and the Supernatural in Halakhic Writing of the Eighteenth Century",
          "authors": "Shweka, Aharon",
          "journal_name": "Jewish History",
          "journal_abbrev": "Jewish Hist",
          "issn": "0334-701X",
          "volume": null,
          "issue": null,
          "pages": null,
          "abstract": "Abstract This essay examines Maoz Kahana’s A Heartless Chicken: Religion and Science in Early Modern Rabbinic Culture . Against familiar narratives that explain change in early modern Judaism primarily through rupture, conflict, and defensive retrenchment, it argues that Kahana uncovers a more expansive intellectual world in which halakhic writing became a site for negotiating science, natural philosophy, and supernatural phenomena. It follows Kahana’s treatment of the “heartless chicken” controversy, bodily anomalies and medical testimony, and discussions of demons, spirits, and other wondrous beings, while advancing several new interpretations. Particular attention is devoted to R. Jonathan Eybeschütz’s consultations with the universities of Prague and Halle. Rather than subordinating Halakhah to external scientific expertise, Eybeschütz enlisted institutional science to confirm established halakhic norms. Yet in cases involving individual suffering or urgent social consequences, he set aside general scientific claims and employed halakhic mechanisms that enabled exceptional rulings. More broadly, the essay emphasizes that theoretical positions expressed in halakhic form should not automatically be read as operative legal rulings or straightforward evidence of a rabbi’s attitude toward science. At a moment when forms of wonder seem to be re-emerging in the age of artificial intelligence, Kahana’s book serves not only as a historical study of eighteenth-century rabbinic culture but also as a suggestive guide to thinking anew about the relation between nature and magic, science and religion.",
          "url": "https://doi.org/10.1007/s10835-026-09498-8",
          "published_online": "2026-08-19T00:00:00",
          "published_print": null,
          "published_date": "2026-08-19T00:00:00",
          "subjects": null,
          "article_type": "journal-article"
        },
